1. What is an infinitive?

Infinitive = to + V1 (base form)

Examples:

to learn
to earn
to forgive
to see
to enter

examples include:

To learn a new language is difficult.

He wishes to earn money quickly.

He comes to see me every day.

Here, the infinitive can function like a noun or express purpose.


2. Bare infinitive

Sometimes we use V1 without “to.”

Important verbs/structures:

make,made,bade, bid, see,saw,except,would rather, sooner than,let,but,than, hear ,heard+ object + V1

Examples:

She made him work. ✅
She made him to work. ❌

Let him go. ✅
Let him to go. ❌

I saw her dance. ✅
I saw her to dance. ❌

I heard him sing. ✅
I heard him to sing. ❌

3. But notice the passive!

This is where students often get confused:

Active:

The teacher made him work.

Passive:

He was made to work.

So:

make + object + V1

but

be made + to + V1

Similarly:

They made me leave. ✅
I was made to leave. ✅

4. Would rather

After would rather, use V1:

I would rather stay home. ✅
I would rather to stay home. ❌

With than, the parallel structure can be:

I would rather go to school than stay at home. ✅

So remember:

would rather + V1

5. “Help” is special

After help, you can use either:

She helped me do the work. ✅

or

She helped me to do the work. ✅

Both are correct.

6. See + object

With see, there are actually different possibilities:

I saw her do the work.
→ I saw the whole action.

I saw her doing the work.
→ I saw her in the middle of the action.

But:

❌ I saw her to do the work.


